  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q4-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Find the equations of two different parabolas that intersect at $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$. There are many possibilities!",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "For example, $y=x^2$ and $y=-(x-1)^2+1$.",
      "worked": "Choose $y=x^2$, which passes through $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$. A different parabola through these points is $y=-(x-1)^2+1$. Equating the two expressions gives\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2&=-(x-1)^2+1\\\\x^2&=-x^2+2x\\\\2x^2-2x&=0\\\\2x(x-1)&=0\\\\x&=0\\text{ or }1.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting into $y=x^2$ gives $y=0$ when $x=0$ and $y=1$ when $x=1$. Thus the intersections are exactly $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 55
  },
